  <!-- 3. 24-48H DELTA -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The Doha technical working group — tasked with advancing Hormuz transit-fee mechanics and nuclear verification scope between the June 22 Lake Lucerne framework session and the High Level Committee's planned inaugural — concluded its July 1 round with both core issues unresolved. Qatar's chief mediator stated only that 'the next meeting will be scheduled at the earliest possible time,' without confirming a date, venue, or delegational composition for the HLC inaugural. The Washington Times and Al Jazeera both carried identical language from Qatari officials on July 1, with no follow-on meeting commitment from either the US or Iranian side. The funeral pause July 4–9 removes five calendar days from the 14-day window before the July 16 horizon deadline. → moved <span class="cite">F9</span></p>

      <p>Parliamentary Speaker Ghalibaf appeared on Iranian state television on July 2 to state that IAEA inspectors 'cannot visit nuclear sites bombed by the US,' naming Fordow, Natanz, and Isfahan by name. Deputy Foreign Minister Gharibabadi followed within hours with a statement carried by IRNA that IAEA access 'will solely be examined and resolved within the framework of a final agreement' — not as a precursor measure. Both statements contradicted VP Vance's June 22 assertion that IAEA inspections were imminent and IAEA DG Grossi's June 24 claim that inspection dates were 'under active discussion.' The dual-official framing on July 2 represents a coordinated hardening of the Iranian position on verification since the Lake Lucerne communiqué. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span></p>

      <p>Hormuz commercial traffic held at approximately 5 vessels per day as of July 1 per NBC News AIS tracker and IMF PortWatch — approximately 5% of the pre-February 2026 baseline of 93 commercial transits per day. CENTCOM's June 16 press release confirmed that mine-countermeasure operations commenced in the Traffic Separation Scheme following the Islamabad MOU; maritime industry analysts cited in Bloomberg and Lloyd's List estimate 40–50 days from initial clearance to the insurer confidence level required for P&amp;I cover reinstatement, placing potential recovery around August 5. No TSS lane had been publicly declared mine-cleared as of July 2, and the IMF PortWatch 7-day average showed no upward trend through the reporting window. → supports <span class="cite">F11</span> without move</p>

      <p>Israeli Defense Forces conducted strikes on Hezbollah logistics routes and command nodes in southern Lebanon on July 1–2. Lebanon's caretaker government filed a formal protest with the UN Security Council. UNIFIL reported no direct engagement with its positions. The strikes occurred within the framework of ongoing IDF operational objectives in Lebanon and did not constitute a direct Iran-Israel military exchange within the July 1–2 reporting window; no IRGC or Iran MFA statement attributed the operations to Iranian direction or claimed retaliatory authority. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>The Knesset legal adviser submitted a formal recommendation to the House Committee on July 2 that the dissolution bill's second reading could not feasibly proceed before July 17 — two days after the <span class="cite">F6</span> deadline. No second-reading vote had been scheduled in the Knesset plenary as of the close of the July 2 session. The Haredi bloc legislative boycott, sustained in connection with the Basic Law Torah Study dispute, has cleared the plenary calendar for the dissolution debate but simultaneously denied it the quorum-sustaining participation that final readings require under Knesset procedure. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

