  <!-- 5. INSIDE IRAN -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRIB's flagship evening bulletin on July 1 characterized the Islamabad MOU as 'a ceasefire, not a concession,' explicitly contrasting the 60-day framework with the 2015 JCPOA. State media avoided the term 'nuclear agreement' in favor of 'humanitarian security compact.' Kayhan's editorial page carried a piece by a named IRGC-affiliated commentator asserting that Iran's HEU stockpile 'was not, is not, and will not be subject to foreign inspection' — language that went beyond the official Gharibabadi statement earlier in the day. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Ghalibaf's July 2 television appearance served dual functions in state-media framing: it restated the IAEA access boundary (no inspections of bombed facilities absent a final deal) and simultaneously presented Hormuz transit fees as a sovereign right grounded in Iranian territorial waters jurisdiction. IRNA carried both as separate lead items, each attributed to a named senior official. Fars News cited the 'Hormuz service fee' position as grounded in UNCLOS Article 26 provisions — a new legal framing that had not appeared in Doha readouts from the July 1 talks. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>

      <p>Press TV's English-language broadcast on July 1–2 framed Israeli strikes on Hezbollah positions as evidence that the Lake Lucerne ceasefire framework 'does not bind Israeli aggression,' with IRIB commentary asserting that Lebanon operations constituted a 'violation of the spirit' of the Islamabad MOU. These frames appeared exclusively in editorial and commentary segments on IRIB and Press TV without MFA or IRGC attribution — no official Iranian statement threatened a response or claimed retaliatory authority in connection with the Lebanon strikes. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

